1
00:00:04,003 --> 00:00:06,590
-What are you working on?
-Can't talk. In the zone.

2
00:00:07,549 --> 00:00:09,384
-Do you know what he's doing?
-Could be anything.

3
00:00:09,551 --> 00:00:11,010
Last time he was like this,
he figured out

4
00:00:11,178 --> 00:00:12,971
electron transport in graphene.

5
00:00:13,138 --> 00:00:15,014
The time before that,
he was making a list of who's allowed

6
00:00:15,182 --> 00:00:17,351
in his tree fort if he ever gets one.

7
00:00:18,519 --> 00:00:20,479
Still can't believe
I didn't make the cut.

8
00:00:22,856 --> 00:00:24,650
Sheldon, you want to take a break?
Your food's ready.

9
00:00:24,816 --> 00:00:28,111
No, what are you doing?
He's both happy and quiet.

10
00:00:28,278 --> 00:00:31,240
It's like seeing a unicorn
and Bigfoot at the same time.

11
00:00:33,116 --> 00:00:34,368
Sorry we're so late.

12
00:00:34,535 --> 00:00:36,161
Bernadette got stuck at work.

13
00:00:36,328 --> 00:00:39,456
Great news! A raccoon virus
just crossed the species barrier

14
00:00:39,623 --> 00:00:42,125
and now can infect humans!

15
00:00:43,168 --> 00:00:44,503
Why is that great news?

16
00:00:44,670 --> 00:00:46,630
In the pharmaceutical business,
we have a saying:

17
00:00:46,797 --> 00:00:48,090
"Mo' infections, mo' money."

18
00:00:49,841 --> 00:00:51,301
Wait!

19
00:00:51,468 --> 00:00:53,804
Maybe you want to wash
your little raccoon hands first.

20
00:00:53,971 --> 00:00:56,557
Calm down.
If I had it, I'd be dead already.

21
00:00:59,685 --> 00:01:00,686
Guys, listen up.

22
00:01:00,852 --> 00:01:01,895
Can't listen, zone.

23
00:01:03,062 --> 00:01:04,690
Also don't care.

24
00:01:05,691 --> 00:01:07,985
Next week is the anniversary
of my first date with Bernadette.

25
00:01:08,151 --> 00:01:10,195
Really don't care.

26
00:01:10,362 --> 00:01:11,697
I want to do something special,

27
00:01:11,863 --> 00:01:13,532
and I was hoping you guys
could be a part of it.

28
00:01:13,699 --> 00:01:16,660
What horrible thing
are you trying to make up for?

29
00:01:16,827 --> 00:01:19,746
Just putting something in the bank
for what horrible thing I do next.

30
00:01:19,913 --> 00:01:20,622
Ah.

31
00:01:22,457 --> 00:01:24,334
Look, she's gonna be back any second,
so here's the deal:

32
00:01:24,501 --> 00:01:27,045
I'm writing a song, and hoping we
could all play it for her together.

33
00:01:27,212 --> 00:01:29,298
-I love that.
-That is so beautiful.

34
00:01:29,464 --> 00:01:30,799
Sheldon?

35
00:01:32,718 --> 00:01:34,261
When did we get
to The Cheesecake Factory?

36
00:01:35,762 --> 00:01:38,223
(THEME SONG PLAYS)

37
00:01:59,786 --> 00:02:02,372
It's really sweet what Howard
wants to do for Bernadette.

38
00:02:02,539 --> 00:02:05,667
Yeah. Hey, how come you've never done
anything romantic to celebrate our first date?

39
00:02:05,834 --> 00:02:07,127
The Romance Resonance

40
00:02:07,544 --> 00:02:09,755
Well, for starters, you've broken up
with me so many times,

41
00:02:09,921 --> 00:02:11,923
which first date
are we talking about?

42
00:02:13,132 --> 00:02:15,552
Whoa! Somebody call
the burn ward.

43
00:02:17,638 --> 00:02:19,306
And back to the zone.

44
00:02:20,599 --> 00:02:23,644
And besides, I do romantic things
for you all the time.

45
00:02:23,810 --> 00:02:26,021
Can you even name one romantic thing
you've done for me?

46
00:02:26,188 --> 00:02:28,815
-I can name tons.
-Sex doesn't count.

47
00:02:30,275 --> 00:02:31,943
I know, what about
that bed-and-breakfast?

48
00:02:32,110 --> 00:02:33,904
Well, I took you there.
All you did was...

49
00:02:34,071 --> 00:02:35,906
I know what I did.

50
00:02:36,948 --> 00:02:39,701
I bet they had to throw out
that rocking chair.

51
00:02:41,411 --> 00:02:43,622
You know, I can be romantic
if I want to.

52
00:02:43,789 --> 00:02:46,708
It's fine. And also not true.

53
00:02:48,293 --> 00:02:49,670
Okay, just you wait and see.

54
00:02:49,836 --> 00:02:52,089
I'm gonna romance
your freakin' ass off.

55
00:02:53,924 --> 00:02:55,634
That's beautiful.
Is that Shakespeare?

56
00:02:56,968 --> 00:02:59,221
-Sheldon?
-Zone.

57
00:03:00,681 --> 00:03:03,141
He'll figure it out
when he falls off the roof.

58
00:03:06,853 --> 00:03:09,231
Sheldon, I know you're in the zone,
but do you want some tea?

59
00:03:13,860 --> 00:03:16,195
All right, I can't believe
I'm gonna say this, but

60
00:03:16,363 --> 00:03:18,699
you haven't spoken in hours
and I'm starting to get worried.

61
00:03:18,865 --> 00:03:20,033
Please say something.

62
00:03:20,199 --> 00:03:24,871
Leonard, prepare to be humbled
and weep at the glory of my genius.

63
00:03:26,373 --> 00:03:28,083
Nope, it was better before.

64
00:03:29,543 --> 00:03:31,002
Look at it.

65
00:03:31,169 --> 00:03:33,630
I feel like my mind just made a baby.

66
00:03:34,923 --> 00:03:37,300
And it's beautiful.

67
00:03:37,467 --> 00:03:40,345
It's not like human babies,
which are loud and covered in goop.

68
00:03:42,180 --> 00:03:45,225
Holy crap, Sheldon,
did you just figure out a method

69
00:03:45,392 --> 00:03:47,978
for synthesizing a new stable
super-heavy element?

70
00:03:48,812 --> 00:03:51,565
Did I?
Well, that can't be right.

71
00:03:51,732 --> 00:03:54,776
No one's ever done that before.
Except me, because I just did it!

72
00:03:55,652 --> 00:03:58,947
Sheldon and his brain, yeah!
Sheldon and his brain, yeah!

73
00:03:59,114 --> 00:04:02,325
Sheldon and his brain, yeah!
Sheldon and his brain, yeah!

74
00:04:02,492 --> 00:04:05,203
- Sheldon and his brain...
-Definitely better before.

75
00:04:07,164 --> 00:04:09,207
So, I'm almost done
writing the song for Bernadette.

76
00:04:09,374 --> 00:04:10,625
Are you cool playing the cello?

77
00:04:10,792 --> 00:04:13,462
If by "cool"
you mean "willing to," yes.

78
00:04:13,628 --> 00:04:15,213
If by "cool" you mean "cool,"

79
00:04:15,380 --> 00:04:17,299
clearly you've never seen me
play the cello.

80
00:04:18,300 --> 00:04:20,135
Great. Will you play the ukulele?

81
00:04:20,302 --> 00:04:23,180
Of course. I'd be happy
to shred it on my ax.

82
00:04:24,681 --> 00:04:28,268
Or you could just play your tiny,
ridiculous guitar.

83
00:04:30,645 --> 00:04:32,522
Fine. I will melt her heart.

84
00:04:33,398 --> 00:04:35,484
And her face!

85
00:04:36,693 --> 00:04:38,069
Gentlemen.

86
00:04:38,236 --> 00:04:40,405
No doubt you heard
about my little breakthrough.

87
00:04:40,572 --> 00:04:43,617
Now, if your plan
is to hoist me on your shoulders

88
00:04:43,784 --> 00:04:45,952
and carry me around the cafeteria,
please refrain.

89
00:04:46,119 --> 00:04:48,455
I don't care for heights,
motion sickness

90
00:04:48,622 --> 00:04:51,124
or the thought of your necks
touching my buttocks.

91
00:04:53,001 --> 00:04:54,711
Seriously, congratulations, Sheldon.

92
00:04:54,878 --> 00:04:56,254
Yeah, I read your paper online.

93
00:04:56,421 --> 00:04:59,090
That technique for creating
a new heavy element is inspired.

94
00:04:59,257 --> 00:05:00,842
Thank you.
Believe it or not,

95
00:05:01,009 --> 00:05:03,303
I just learned
a Chinese research team

96
00:05:03,470 --> 00:05:05,222
at the Hubei Institute
for Nuclear Physics

97
00:05:05,388 --> 00:05:07,015
ran a test on a cyclotron,

98
00:05:07,182 --> 00:05:08,892
and the results
were extremely promising.

99
00:05:09,059 --> 00:05:10,602
Sheldon, that's incredible.

100
00:05:10,769 --> 00:05:13,021
I know! They called it
the greatest thing

101
00:05:13,188 --> 00:05:14,815
since the Communist party.

102
00:05:16,107 --> 00:05:18,652
Although I'm pretty sure that the
Communist party made them say that.

103
00:05:20,237 --> 00:05:21,613
I like China.

104
00:05:21,780 --> 00:05:23,907
See, they know how
to keep people in line.

105
00:05:25,617 --> 00:05:26,952
So, what happens next?

106
00:05:27,118 --> 00:05:29,788
More testing,
more success, more fame.

107
00:05:29,955 --> 00:05:33,875
But don't worry, I will remain
the same down-to-earth humble Joe

108
00:05:34,042 --> 00:05:37,128
-I've always been.
-Good to know.

109
00:05:37,295 --> 00:05:39,798
Now give me that cookie;
I discovered an element.

110
00:05:43,093 --> 00:05:44,803
Thank you so much for coming.

111
00:05:44,970 --> 00:05:46,930
You called the right person.

112
00:05:47,097 --> 00:05:49,348
I believe I have the perfect romantic
evening for you to give Leonard.

113
00:05:49,516 --> 00:05:52,018
Okay, good,
'cause I've been really struggling with this.

114
00:05:52,185 --> 00:05:53,352
As I'm sure you're aware,

115
00:05:53,520 --> 00:05:56,022
the quickest way to a man's heart
is through his...

116
00:05:56,189 --> 00:05:58,859
Pants, but Leonard says
sex doesn't count.

117
00:06:00,235 --> 00:06:01,862
Hm. You poor thing.

118
00:06:02,028 --> 00:06:04,322
You have one arrow in your quiver,
and you just can't use it.

119
00:06:05,615 --> 00:06:08,535
Fortunately, another pathway
to a man's heart

120
00:06:08,702 --> 00:06:11,121
is through his stomach.

121
00:06:11,288 --> 00:06:13,623
I don't know if I want to cook
for him; he's kind of a picky eater.

122
00:06:13,790 --> 00:06:16,418
I mean, "It's too salty.
It's too dry.

123
00:06:16,585 --> 00:06:19,296
It's too burnt and frozen
at the same time."

124
00:06:21,172 --> 00:06:22,883
Wait, come on, what else
would sweep you off your feet?

125
00:06:23,049 --> 00:06:26,761
Well, I've always had this fantasy
that involves dancing.

126
00:06:26,928 --> 00:06:31,725
The sexual chemistry between
my partner and me is electric.

127
00:06:31,892 --> 00:06:34,185
But boy, oh, boy,
does my father not approve

128
00:06:34,352 --> 00:06:37,147
until he sees us
in the big dance competition.

129
00:06:38,523 --> 00:06:40,275
Okay, that's just the plot
for Dirty Dancing.

130
00:06:41,860 --> 00:06:43,528
What else would you love,
other than

131
00:06:43,695 --> 00:06:46,489
being lifted
over Patrick Swayze's head?

132
00:06:46,656 --> 00:06:49,868
Oh, oh. You could
stand outside his window

133
00:06:50,035 --> 00:06:52,287
with a boom box in the air.

134
00:06:54,873 --> 00:06:56,666
That's from Say Anything.

135
00:06:57,834 --> 00:07:00,295
Look, I'm a lonely guy;
I watch a lot of movies.

136
00:07:01,630 --> 00:07:02,797
Look, Penny,

137
00:07:02,964 --> 00:07:06,092
if you truly want to be romantic,
it needs to come from you.

138
00:07:06,259 --> 00:07:08,303
I get that, but why is this so hard?

139
00:07:08,470 --> 00:07:10,972
Well, you've probably never had
to do this stuff 'cause you're young

140
00:07:11,139 --> 00:07:13,266
and beautiful and men have
always thrown themselves at you.

141
00:07:15,936 --> 00:07:18,438
Yeah, I'm trying to be sad
about that. I can't.

142
00:07:22,943 --> 00:07:26,029
Let's see, what's next?
Okay! Here.

143
00:07:26,196 --> 00:07:27,989
This is the Magic Marker

144
00:07:28,156 --> 00:07:30,367
I was using
when I made the discovery.

145
00:07:32,285 --> 00:07:34,329
I don't think the Smithsonian's
gonna want your marker.

146
00:07:34,496 --> 00:07:37,791
And that's why you're not
on the list for my tree fort.

147
00:07:38,875 --> 00:07:40,543
(CELL PHONE VIBRATING)

148
00:07:41,711 --> 00:07:43,755
Ooh! Guess who's getting an article

149
00:07:43,922 --> 00:07:45,423
written about him in Physics Today?

150
00:07:45,590 --> 00:07:47,050
I'll give you a hint.

151
00:07:47,217 --> 00:07:50,095
You measured the diameter
of his suspicious mole yesterday.

152
00:07:52,222 --> 00:07:53,556
Sheldon, I'm so proud of you.

153
00:07:53,723 --> 00:07:56,810
Well, you should be. My discovery
is spreading like wildfire.

154
00:07:56,977 --> 00:08:00,188
Unlike my mole, which is holding
steady at the size of a navy bean.

155
00:08:02,315 --> 00:08:03,316
What's next?

156
00:08:03,483 --> 00:08:05,318
This is the very copy

157
00:08:05,484 --> 00:08:07,654
of The Handbook
of Chemistry and Physics

158
00:08:07,821 --> 00:08:11,157
in which I looked up the reaction
rates of mendelevium and....

159
00:08:13,284 --> 00:08:14,661
-And what?
-No.

160
00:08:16,037 --> 00:08:18,665
-No, no, no, no.
-What's wrong?

161
00:08:21,084 --> 00:08:22,836
I've made a horrible mistake.

162
00:08:23,003 --> 00:08:24,045
What are you talking about?

163
00:08:24,212 --> 00:08:27,173
This table,
it's in square centimeters.

164
00:08:27,340 --> 00:08:28,883
I read it as square meters.

165
00:08:29,050 --> 00:08:30,260
You know what that means?

166
00:08:30,427 --> 00:08:32,679
That Americans can't handle
the metric system?

167
00:08:35,390 --> 00:08:38,309
Amy, I was off by a factor of 10,000.

168
00:08:39,185 --> 00:08:41,021
But the Chinese team
found the element.

169
00:08:41,187 --> 00:08:44,024
They shouldn't have.
My calculations were wrong.

170
00:08:44,190 --> 00:08:46,901
There must be some resonance between
the elements I didn't know about.

171
00:08:47,068 --> 00:08:48,653
So you just got lucky?

172
00:08:48,820 --> 00:08:51,740
Sheldon Cooper does not get lucky!

173
00:08:53,450 --> 00:08:55,326
You and me both, brother.

174
00:08:57,078 --> 00:08:59,247
It doesn't matter.
The element was found because of you,

175
00:08:59,414 --> 00:09:00,665
and that's groundbreaking.

176
00:09:00,832 --> 00:09:03,376
What matters is
the greatest scientific achievement

177
00:09:03,543 --> 00:09:05,712
of my life is based on a blunder.

178
00:09:05,879 --> 00:09:08,339
I'm not a genius, I'm a fraud.

179
00:09:09,841 --> 00:09:11,051
You know, Sheldon, in neuroscience,

180
00:09:11,217 --> 00:09:13,470
we're forever finding something
in one part of the brain

181
00:09:13,636 --> 00:09:14,721
that we thought was someplace else.

182
00:09:14,888 --> 00:09:19,350
Great. Now I'm worse than
a fraud. I'm practically a biologist.

183
00:09:24,939 --> 00:09:26,733
You got to stop beating
yourself up over this.

184
00:09:26,900 --> 00:09:29,235
I mean, you made a mistake,
but it was a happy mistake.

185
00:09:29,402 --> 00:09:31,321
There's nothing happy about it.

186
00:09:31,488 --> 00:09:33,907
I'm being given credit
that I don't deserve.

187
00:09:34,074 --> 00:09:36,367
People get things
they don't deserve all the time.

188
00:09:37,160 --> 00:09:39,120
Look at me with you.

189
00:09:39,746 --> 00:09:42,415
No, Leonard, this is going
to haunt me for the rest of my life:

190
00:09:42,582 --> 00:09:46,920
every science classroom, every
periodic table, every textbook.

191
00:09:47,087 --> 00:09:49,923
This mistake
will be staring back at me

192
00:09:50,090 --> 00:09:53,384
Like that time you let Koothrappali
wax your eyebrows.

193
00:09:55,720 --> 00:09:57,722
I've got to find a way
to stop this thing.

194
00:09:57,889 --> 00:10:01,476
Buddy, I don't think you can. I mean,
once it's out there, it's out there.

195
00:10:01,643 --> 00:10:04,896
This thing is like the science
equivalent of a sex tape.

196
00:10:06,523 --> 00:10:08,149
Frankly, I'd prefer a sex tape.

197
00:10:08,316 --> 00:10:09,943
You don't know
what a sex tape is, do you?

198
00:10:10,110 --> 00:10:11,194
No.

199
00:10:14,364 --> 00:10:15,782
Make sure you guys get
to the restaurant by 8:00.

200
00:10:15,949 --> 00:10:17,325
Bernadette's
meeting me there at 8:30.

201
00:10:17,492 --> 00:10:19,285
-I don't want to blow the surprise.
-We'll be there.

202
00:10:19,452 --> 00:10:22,914
Got it. Now, I have
a question about the song.

203
00:10:23,081 --> 00:10:26,543
I was thinking at some point
I could bust out a little rap.

204
00:10:28,670 --> 00:10:30,421
Nah, nah, nuh, nuh, nuh, no.

205
00:10:34,092 --> 00:10:36,511
Come on, come on,
you haven't even heard it yet.

206
00:10:36,678 --> 00:10:37,971
Leonard, give me a beat.

207
00:10:38,138 --> 00:10:39,889
I will not.

208
00:10:41,266 --> 00:10:42,350
Please?

209
00:10:42,517 --> 00:10:45,061
-No, I want this to be romantic.
-It will be!

210
00:10:45,228 --> 00:10:47,689
I don't call anyone a ho,
and the only time

211
00:10:47,856 --> 00:10:50,567
I use the phrase "my bitch,"
I'm referring to you.

212
00:10:52,527 --> 00:10:54,696
MAN: There he is!
(APPLAUSE)

213
00:10:56,489 --> 00:10:59,117
Stop it. Stop celebrating me!

214
00:10:59,742 --> 00:11:00,994
Woo!

215
00:11:01,161 --> 00:11:03,204
And no woos!

216
00:11:03,371 --> 00:11:04,622
Not you, Dr. Woo, you're fine.

217
00:11:09,210 --> 00:11:12,297
I want you all to know that
you have no reason to applaud me.

218
00:11:12,463 --> 00:11:16,092
My so-called breakthrough
wasn't the result of my genius.

219
00:11:16,259 --> 00:11:18,595
It was nothing more
than a boneheaded mistake.

220
00:11:18,761 --> 00:11:22,348
So please refrain from praising me
for it in the future.

221
00:11:22,932 --> 00:11:24,683
I don't understand.
They didn't find the element?

222
00:11:24,851 --> 00:11:26,769
No, they found the element.

223
00:11:26,936 --> 00:11:29,355
(APPLAUSE)
No, no, stop it!

224
00:11:29,522 --> 00:11:31,608
I don't need to take this admiration
from the likes of you people!

225
00:11:33,902 --> 00:11:37,238
-How do I make them stop loving me?
-Invite them to live with us.

226
00:11:41,367 --> 00:11:43,411
Boy, Bernadette is gonna love this.

227
00:11:43,578 --> 00:11:47,457
It must be nice to have someone
do something so romantic.

228
00:11:48,291 --> 00:11:49,792
Okay, you know what's not romantic?

229
00:11:49,959 --> 00:11:51,377
Rubbing it in someone's face.

230
00:11:51,544 --> 00:11:54,713
Actually, it can be,
but I told you sex doesn't count.

231
00:11:57,675 --> 00:12:01,095
I'm getting worried about Bernadette.
I'm gonna call and check on her.

232
00:12:01,720 --> 00:12:05,058
The National Science Foundation
wants to give me a substantial grant.

233
00:12:05,225 --> 00:12:07,518
-That's a big deal.
-I know.

234
00:12:07,685 --> 00:12:09,520
When will this nightmare end?

235
00:12:10,772 --> 00:12:12,774
Hey, I get that you feel bad about
all the attention,

236
00:12:12,941 --> 00:12:15,944
but still, what you did is amazing.
We're really proud of you.

237
00:12:16,110 --> 00:12:19,113
-I'm not.
-You're not?

238
00:12:19,280 --> 00:12:21,824
Sheldon, I've been thinking about it,

239
00:12:21,991 --> 00:12:24,619
and you're right,
you don't deserve any credit.

240
00:12:25,495 --> 00:12:28,081
All you did was misread
some numbers on a table.

241
00:12:28,248 --> 00:12:29,624
A very easy table, too.

242
00:12:29,791 --> 00:12:32,877
Honestly, I'm embarrassed for you.

243
00:12:35,213 --> 00:12:37,340
That's the most romantic thing
I've ever heard.

244
00:12:39,300 --> 00:12:42,553
Damn it, everyone's
better at this than me.

245
00:12:42,720 --> 00:12:46,140
Congratulations, Dr. Fowler.
You just made the fort.

246
00:12:49,852 --> 00:12:51,813
Guys, there was an accident
at Bernie's lab.

247
00:12:51,980 --> 00:12:53,356
My God, is she okay?

248
00:12:53,523 --> 00:12:55,858
Yeah, but she's at the hospital
in quarantine.

249
00:12:56,025 --> 00:12:57,652
-What?
-Poor Bernadette.

250
00:12:57,819 --> 00:13:01,155
I hate to pile on the bad news,
but I just got a raise.

251
00:13:09,998 --> 00:13:12,583
My God, Bernie, what happened?

252
00:13:12,750 --> 00:13:15,295
Well, let's just say the next time
you move a dozen vials

253
00:13:15,461 --> 00:13:18,506
of raccoon virus to the fridge,
make two trips.

254
00:13:20,633 --> 00:13:21,800
You're sure you're okay?

255
00:13:21,968 --> 00:13:24,137
Yeah, it's just a precaution.
If there was a problem,

256
00:13:24,304 --> 00:13:26,681
I'd be throwing up
out of my eyeballs by now.

257
00:13:28,725 --> 00:13:31,227
-Sorry I messed up our date.
-No, don't worry about it.

258
00:13:31,394 --> 00:13:33,062
I brought the date here.

259
00:13:33,229 --> 00:13:34,856
Guys, come on in.

260
00:13:35,982 --> 00:13:36,983
What's all this?

261
00:13:37,150 --> 00:13:40,570
Well, tonight is the anniversary
of our first date,

262
00:13:40,737 --> 00:13:44,324
and I wanted to celebrate it
by writing a song for you.

263
00:13:44,490 --> 00:13:45,616
Howie.

264
00:13:45,783 --> 00:13:47,535
Sheldon, get over here!

265
00:13:48,453 --> 00:13:50,038
She might be contagious.

266
00:13:51,539 --> 00:13:53,624
Don't you think I'm having
a rough enough day?

267
00:13:54,250 --> 00:13:55,251
Sheldon!

268
00:14:03,718 --> 00:14:05,553
(PLAYING UPBEAT SONG
ON KEYBOARD)

269
00:14:06,929 --> 00:14:08,514
If I didn't have you

270
00:14:08,681 --> 00:14:10,433
Life would be blue

271
00:14:10,600 --> 00:14:13,561
I'd be Doctor Who
without the TARDIS

272
00:14:13,728 --> 00:14:16,064
Is it me,
or does she not look so good?

273
00:14:17,065 --> 00:14:19,650
A candle without a wick
A Watson without a Crick

274
00:14:19,817 --> 00:14:22,695
I'd be one of my outfits
without a dickey

275
00:14:22,862 --> 00:14:24,697
I'd be cheese without the mac

276
00:14:24,863 --> 00:14:27,450
Jobs without the Wozniak

277
00:14:27,617 --> 00:14:28,910
I'd be solving
exponential equations

278
00:14:29,077 --> 00:14:30,495
That use bases
not found on your calculator

279
00:14:30,661 --> 00:14:33,039
Making it much harder to crack

280
00:14:33,206 --> 00:14:35,124
I'd be an atom without a bomb

281
00:14:35,291 --> 00:14:36,793
A dot without the com

282
00:14:36,959 --> 00:14:39,420
And I'd probably still live
with my mom

283
00:14:40,296 --> 00:14:43,800
And he'd probably still live
with his mom

284
00:14:43,966 --> 00:14:47,053
Ever since I met you,
you turned my world around

285
00:14:47,220 --> 00:14:50,223
You supported all my dreams
and all my hopes

286
00:14:50,390 --> 00:14:53,309
You're like uranium-235
and I'm uranium-238

287
00:14:53,476 --> 00:14:58,272
Almost inseparable isotopes

288
00:15:00,817 --> 00:15:03,486
I couldn't have imagined

289
00:15:03,653 --> 00:15:06,280
How good my life would get

290
00:15:07,031 --> 00:15:08,032
From the moment

291
00:15:08,199 --> 00:15:10,827
That I met you, Bernadette

292
00:15:10,993 --> 00:15:12,370
Howie.

293
00:15:12,537 --> 00:15:14,956
If I didn't have you

294
00:15:15,123 --> 00:15:16,582
Life would be dreary

295
00:15:16,749 --> 00:15:19,585
I'd be string theory
without any string

296
00:15:19,752 --> 00:15:21,129
I'd be binary code
without a one

297
00:15:21,295 --> 00:15:22,921
A cathode ray tube
without an electron gun

298
00:15:23,089 --> 00:15:25,842
I'd be Firefly, Buffy and Avengers
without Joss Whedon

299
00:15:26,008 --> 00:15:27,760
I'd speak a lot more Klingon

300
00:15:28,094 --> 00:15:30,263
(SINGS IN KLINGON)

301
00:15:31,347 --> 00:15:34,392
And he'd definitely
still live with his mom

302
00:15:35,685 --> 00:15:38,438
Ever since I met you
you turned my world around

303
00:15:38,604 --> 00:15:41,691
You're my best friend
and my lover

304
00:15:41,858 --> 00:15:45,361
We're like changing electric
and magnetic fields

305
00:15:45,528 --> 00:15:47,655
You can't have one

306
00:15:48,656 --> 00:15:51,742
Without the other

307
00:15:53,703 --> 00:15:56,122
I couldn't have imagined

308
00:15:56,289 --> 00:15:59,542
How good my life would get

309
00:15:59,709 --> 00:16:02,795
From the moment that I met you

310
00:16:02,962 --> 00:16:04,338
Bernadette

311
00:16:05,840 --> 00:16:09,510
We couldn't have imagined

312
00:16:09,677 --> 00:16:12,847
How good our lives would get

313
00:16:13,014 --> 00:16:16,225
From the moment that we met you

314
00:16:16,392 --> 00:16:19,604
Bernadette

315
00:16:26,527 --> 00:16:29,822
Howie, that was amazing!
Look, I'm shaking.

316
00:16:29,988 --> 00:16:31,991
She's sick, I knew it. Bye!

317
00:16:37,121 --> 00:16:40,374
I have to say, this is the best
Top Ramen you've ever made.

318
00:16:41,667 --> 00:16:45,129
I discovered a secret ingredient:
the flavor packet.

319
00:16:46,714 --> 00:16:48,591
That sucker is well named.

320
00:16:51,636 --> 00:16:53,012
All right, lover boy, get ready,

321
00:16:53,179 --> 00:16:56,557
'cause there is a crap storm
of romance coming your way.

322
00:16:56,724 --> 00:16:58,809
Stop it, you're gonna make me cry.

323
00:17:00,311 --> 00:17:01,729
(ROMANTIC MUSIC PLAYS)

324
00:17:01,896 --> 00:17:03,731
All right, you seem pretty confident.

325
00:17:03,898 --> 00:17:05,107
Oh, I am.

326
00:17:06,234 --> 00:17:08,986
Maybe if you follow this trail,

327
00:17:09,153 --> 00:17:10,530
you'll see why.

328
00:17:10,696 --> 00:17:13,282
-Oh... Rose petals.
-Yes.

329
00:17:13,449 --> 00:17:16,661
The most beautiful and shockingly
expensive of all the flowers.

330
00:17:19,997 --> 00:17:23,041
You made the bed? You really
are pulling out all the stops.

331
00:17:24,460 --> 00:17:26,587
Okay, I gave this a lot of thought,

332
00:17:27,505 --> 00:17:30,758
and I finally found something
to show you how much I love you.

333
00:17:35,513 --> 00:17:37,348
Oh, wow.

334
00:17:37,515 --> 00:17:40,851
It's a first edition
of Hitchhiker's Guide to the Galaxy.

335
00:17:41,018 --> 00:17:43,437
I remember it was your
favorite book growing up.

336
00:17:44,564 --> 00:17:46,357
This is great.

337
00:17:46,524 --> 00:17:48,609
Thank you... so much.

338
00:17:49,902 --> 00:17:51,362
What?

339
00:17:51,529 --> 00:17:53,030
Nothing. I love it.

340
00:17:53,197 --> 00:17:55,283
No, something's going on.
What's wrong with it?

341
00:17:55,449 --> 00:17:57,785
I remember you saying how great
it would be to have a first edition.

342
00:17:58,536 --> 00:18:01,414
It's true, I did. I did say that...

343
00:18:01,581 --> 00:18:03,916
...when we were at
the used bookstore together

344
00:18:04,082 --> 00:18:07,044
and I saw the first edition
and I bought it.

345
00:18:08,754 --> 00:18:11,716
Oh my God, I am the worst!

346
00:18:11,882 --> 00:18:13,426
No, no, it's okay.
It's really thoughtful.

347
00:18:13,593 --> 00:18:15,261
No, it's not! I mean...

348
00:18:16,512 --> 00:18:18,806
What's thoughtful
is everything you do.

349
00:18:20,933 --> 00:18:22,935
Here, you know what?
Look at this.

350
00:18:24,729 --> 00:18:26,897
Look, here's the...
the plane ticket you bought me

351
00:18:27,064 --> 00:18:29,275
when I was too poor
to go home for the holidays.

352
00:18:29,442 --> 00:18:33,446
And the rose you left
on my windshield... just because.

353
00:18:34,322 --> 00:18:36,198
Here's the thank-you letter
you wrote me

354
00:18:36,365 --> 00:18:37,950
after the first time
I slept with you.

355
00:18:39,619 --> 00:18:42,288
All 11 pages of it.

356
00:18:44,665 --> 00:18:46,334
Can't believe
you saved all this stuff.

357
00:18:47,960 --> 00:18:50,254
Of course I did. It's you.

358
00:18:52,673 --> 00:18:54,008
Come here.

359
00:18:58,846 --> 00:19:01,098
Is that a pregnancy test?

360
00:19:01,265 --> 00:19:03,809
Just the first one.
I didn't save them all.

361
00:19:07,938 --> 00:19:10,983
I can't believe
I read this table wrong.

362
00:19:11,149 --> 00:19:12,526
I blame you.

363
00:19:14,654 --> 00:19:15,988
Me? What did I do?

364
00:19:16,155 --> 00:19:17,531
You distract me.

365
00:19:17,698 --> 00:19:20,117
I've been distracted
since the moment I met you.

366
00:19:22,328 --> 00:19:24,538
-Sorry.
-Well, you should be.

367
00:19:24,705 --> 00:19:28,250
Because all I can think about
is how much I want to kiss you.

368
00:19:31,379 --> 00:19:33,673
And not just on the cheek,
but on the mouth.

369
00:19:37,385 --> 00:19:39,470
Like mommies and daddies do.

370
00:19:43,349 --> 00:19:44,850
Oh, Sheldon.

371
00:19:50,022 --> 00:19:51,273
SHELDON: Amy?

372
00:19:53,943 --> 00:19:57,154
Amy? Did you hear what I said?

373
00:19:57,321 --> 00:19:58,823
Can't talk, in the zone.

